---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/paths';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { site, libraries } from '../config';
import { count } from '../lib/catalog';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description={`The Senan Shaibani Cultural Center is a research collection in Middle Eastern studies in ${site.location}.`}
>
  <h1>About the center</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The Senan Shaibani Cultural Center is a working research collection in
    Middle Eastern studies, assembled from several scholarly libraries and
    held at a single site in {site.location}, forty-five miles east of Austin.
  </p>

  <p>
    The center exists to keep a body of material available to the people who
    need it. Much of what it holds — Arabic-language periodicals from the
    1950s and 1960s, Arab American diaspora publications, runs of journals
    that ceased decades ago — survives in few other places in the United
    States, and in some cases nowhere else. Making it findable is the whole of
    the work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Holdings are consultable on site by arrangement. The catalog is public,
    and currently describes {count.toLocaleString()} items; cataloging of the
    remaining collections is ongoing.
  </p>

  <h2>Senan Shaibani</h2>

  <Figure
    src={url('/img/senan.jpg')}
    alt="Senan Shaibani, a young bearded man, smiling in front of a flowering bougainvillea."
    caption="Senan Shaibani."
    portrait
    width={620}
    height={887}
  />

  <p>
    Senan Shaibani devoted his life to education and cultural dialogue. His
    studies in Middle Eastern thought and his dedication to human rights
    shaped a vision of knowledge as both a right and a responsibility.
    Through his writing, teaching, and activism, Senan inspired those around
    him to see learning as a shared pursuit. This center continues his legacy
    by creating a space where ideas can meet, traditions can be explored, and
    new understanding can emerge.
  </p>

  <!--
    EDITORIAL NOTE — not published, remove once filled in.

    This section still needs real biography written by the family: dates,
    education, work, and what he read and collected. The migration source
    contained a single sentence.

    A page of substance here is what keeps the rest of the site scholarly —
    the commemorative weight of the project belongs on this page and not
    distributed through the catalog or the collections.

    If the Marcel Khalife dedication is to be mentioned, it belongs here, as a
    sentence of biography rather than as embedded video.
  -->

  <h2>The collections</h2>

  <p>
    The center's holdings come from three named collections, each reflecting
    the working library of the scholars who assembled it.
  </p>

  <dl class="libraries">
    {
      libraries.map((library) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{library.name}</dt>
          <dd>{library.summary}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>Cataloging</h2>

  <p>
    Records are created in Zotero against WorldCat, following a
    minimal-processing approach that preserves original order where possible.
    Each record carries shelf-level location, and Arabic titles are recorded in
    both Arabic script and romanized form where both are known.
  </p>

  <p>
    Descriptive records are published under a{' '}<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="license noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>{' '}license and may be reused with attribution. The physical holdings they
    describe may remain in copyright.
  </p>

  <p>
    The catalog is a work in progress and is corrected continually.
    Corrections and additions are welcome at{' '}<a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  h2 { margin-top: 2.5em; }

  .libraries { margin: 1.5rem 0 0; }
  .libraries div + div { margin-top: 1.4rem; }
  .libraries dt { font-size: 1.06rem; margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
  .libraries dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-muted); }
</style>
